From a567963c7f3358eef7c032e3295f129fc4dc2599 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Kiril Swenson <john@blender.org>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 08:12:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] UI: Support holding a shortcut to temporarily switch
 tools

Clicking back and forth in the toolbar to alternate between tools can be
quite slow. A dedicated shortcut with `tool_set_by_id` helps, but it
still requires remembering to switch back to the previous tool every
time (and learning two shortcuts). It would be much more helpful if
Blender did this extra step for the user.

For cases where there is a good "default" (like the select tool), this
adds a new `WM_OT_tool_set_by_id_hold` operator which enables a tool on
press (and hold) of some keyboard shortcut, and returns to the previous
tool on release.

We simply store the previous tool's idname on invoke, execute the tool
change, and run modally until either:
a). the key is released, where we restore the previous tool
b). things were canceled by e.g. closing the window, where we also
restore
c). another `_set_by_id_hold` is run on top of the current one, where we
make sure to _avoid_ overwriting the original idname to restore to, and
instead just finish this operator in favor of the new `_set_by_id_hold`
operator call.

This will be used in !161031 (VSE blade tool rewrite) as a "quick tool".
But other modules and users can also benefit from it, using the operator
if they'd like.

Note that toolsettings can still be adjusted in the N-panel while
holding down the tool shortcut. If one wishes to temporarily make the
tool more "persistent" they can click the tool in the sidebar or enable
it from the Toolbar Popup (as before).

Pull Request: https://projects.blender.org/blender/blender/pulls/164536

Co-authored-by: Campbell Barton <campbell@blender.org>
